{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EyyeUg5oGmovPe4ymgpebg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01G3xUP4eG71JYpL57VCg7WA","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T141728/median/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[review]{acl}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{latexsym}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{microtype}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\n\\title{Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks:\\\\A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that a score on MMLU today reflects the same construct as a score measured a year ago.\nWe test this assumption using Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis on the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe find that 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large DIF (ETS C class, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts---robust across threshold choices (10--49\\%), unaffected by multiple testing correction, and not explained by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\% of variance).\nCrucially, instability does not threaten validity: rankings are extremely robust ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$) to removing all unstable items, revealing massive measurement redundancy.\nWe show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth cannot capture this differential signal (enrichment$\\approx$1.0) and provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000).\nReplication across six benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K, HellaSwag, ARC, WinoGrande, TruthfulQA) confirms C\\% ranges from 17.9\\% to 31.3\\%, with all random controls below 1\\%.\nDIF serves as a diagnostic lens for understanding which capability dimensions shift across model generations, not a quality filter---we propose a concrete audit protocol for benchmark maintenance.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhen we state that ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same latent construct.\nThis assumption of \\emph{temporal score comparability} underpins all cross-time benchmark comparisons, yet it has never been systematically tested at the item level for LLM benchmarks.\n\nPsychometrics provides a mature tool for exactly this test: Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability \\cite{Holland1993}.\nAn item exhibits DIF when two groups with equal total ability have systematically different probabilities of answering it correctly---indicating that the item measures something beyond the intended construct for at least one group.\n\nWe apply Mantel-Haenszel DIF analysis \\cite{Holland1988} to the METABENCH response matrix \\cite{Perlitz2024}---5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items---splitting models into 2023 (reference, $N{=}1{,}080$) and 2024-H1 (focal, $N{=}807$) temporal cohorts.\nOur central finding: \\textbf{31.3\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable} across model generations (ETS C class, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$).\n\n\\paragraph{Instability $\\neq$ Invalidity.}\nThis framing is essential.\nDIF flags items whose functioning \\emph{changed} across generations---this is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict.\nMMLU rankings are massively redundant: the rank-order correlation between full and stable-item-only rankings is $\\rho \\approx 0.997$.\nThe 31.3\\% instability rate does not threaten benchmark validity; rather, it reveals \\emph{which} capability dimensions are shifting across model generations.\n\nWe make four contributions:\n\\begin{enumerate}\\setlength\\itemsep{0pt}\n\\item \\textbf{Empirical discovery at unprecedented scale}: 31.3\\% temporal instability across 12,508 MMLU items, robust to threshold choice, multiple testing, and architecture composition, replicated across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\\%).\n\\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Enrichment$\\approx$1.0 against Li et al.\\ labels is expected, not failure---any item-level detector validated against such labels shares this blind spot.\n\\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: The $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) provides reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level detector, portable across benchmarks (GSM8K $\\Delta$TPR$=+$0.818).\n\\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens}: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal ($z{=}{-}8$ to ${-}49\\sigma$ vs.\\ random removal) but are the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy ($z{=}{-}9.5$ vs.\\ accuracy-gap $z{=}{-}157$). Item-level decomposition reveals a Simpson's paradox: domain improvement predicts DIF marginally (OR$=$1.55), but this reverses (OR$=$0.73) after controlling for item properties.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{LLM Benchmark Contamination.}\nMultiple methods target benchmark contamination at the model level: Min-K\\% \\cite{Shi2024}, ConStat \\cite{Blebea2024}, and n-gram overlap approaches \\cite{Dodge2021,Li2024contamination} all rely on web-overlap or memorization-based labels as validation ground truth.\nThese labels measure whether an item is publicly accessible---global exposure---rather than whether it functions differently across model cohorts.\nOur work shows this distinction matters empirically.\n\n\\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}\nMH-DIF \\cite{Holland1988,Mantel1959} is the standard method in educational testing for detecting items that function differently across examinee groups.\nThe ETS classification system \\cite{Zieky1993} categorizes items into A (negligible), B (moderate), and C (large) DIF.\nWe apply this classical framework without modification; our contribution is the empirical findings at unprecedented scale, not a methodological innovation.\n\n\\paragraph{LLM Benchmark Analysis.}\nRecent work examines benchmark properties through multiple lenses: item difficulty distributions \\cite{Polo2024}, benchmark saturation \\cite{Kiela2021}, and evaluation methodology \\cite{Liang2023}.\nGrowing Pains \\cite{Ivison2024} uses anchor items for efficient evaluation---our DIF diagnostic complements this by identifying which anchor items may be degrading.\nMETABENCH \\cite{Perlitz2024} provides the large-scale response matrix that enables our analysis.\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF Analysis}\n\nFor each item $i$, models are stratified by total score into $K$ ability bins.\nWithin each stratum $k$, a $2 \\times 2$ contingency table cross-tabulates group membership (reference vs.\\ focal) with item response (correct vs.\\ incorrect).\nThe MH odds ratio is:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_{\\text{MH},i} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / T_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / T_k}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are cell counts and $T_k$ is the stratum total.\nThe MH delta statistic $\\Delta_{\\text{MH},i} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\text{MH},i})$ is classified via ETS thresholds: $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.0$ (A, negligible), $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.5$ (B, moderate), $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$ (C, large).\n\nWe define \\textbf{C\\%} as the proportion of items classified ETS C.\nCohort definition: reference $=$ models released before 2024-01-01 ($N{=}1{,}080$), focal $=$ models released 2024-01-01 to 2024-06-30 ($N{=}807$).\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\nTo evaluate DIF detection power under controlled ground truth, we inject known differential contamination into real METABENCH response matrices.\nFor $N_{\\text{inject}}$ items in the focal group, we flip incorrect responses to correct with exploitation rate $p \\in \\{0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$.\nDetection power is measured as $\\Delta\\text{TPR}(p) = \\text{TPR}(p) - \\text{TPR}(0)$, isolating the DIF sensitivity to injected contamination above baseline.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-Subject Matching.}\nA critical methodological innovation: when computing MH-DIF for items in subject $s$, we use the total score on \\emph{all other subjects} as the matching variable.\nThis eliminates matching variable contamination---injected items inflate scores on the target subject, biasing within-subject matching.\nCross-subject matching yields $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 across all tested subjects, while within-subject matching suffers FPR inflation of $+$0.186.\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:temporal_dif}\n\n\\paragraph{Core Result.}\nApplying MH-DIF to the full METABENCH MMLU matrix (5,227 models, 12,508 items) with temporal cohort split yields C\\%$=$31.3\\%, with 20.5\\% B-class and 48.2\\% A-class items (Table~\\ref{tab:temporal_dif}).\nThe mean $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ for C-class items is 2.14 (median 1.78).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Temporal} & \\textbf{Random Split} \\\\\n\\midrule\n$N_{\\text{ref}}$ / $N_{\\text{foc}}$ & 1,080 / 807 & 1,080 / 807 \\\\\nETS A (\\%) & 48.2 & 99.6 \\\\\nETS B (\\%) & 20.5 & 0.2 \\\\\nETS C (\\%) & \\textbf{31.3} & \\textbf{0.18} \\\\\nMean $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ (C items) & 2.14 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Temporal DIF on MMLU. The random split sanity check confirms that 31.3\\% C-class items is far above chance (0.18\\%).}\n\\label{tab:temporal_dif}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Permutation Null.}\nWe run 1,000 permutations randomly reassigning temporal labels while preserving group sizes.\nThe null distribution has mean C\\%$=$0.47\\% (SD$=$0.13\\%), with 99th percentile at 0.84\\%.\nThe observed 31.3\\% yields $z{=}237.2$ ($p{<}0.001$; all 1,000 permutations below observed), corresponding to a 66.6$\\times$ enrichment over the null mean.\n\n\\paragraph{Robustness Checks.}\n\\emph{Threshold sensitivity}: C\\% at $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ thresholds \\{1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5\\} $=$ \\{48.9\\%, 31.3\\%, 18.3\\%, 10.2\\%\\}---substantial instability holds across all standard thresholds.\n\\emph{Multiple testing}: Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction at $q{=}0.05$ removes only 3 of 3,918 C-class items (31.32\\%$\\to$31.30\\%).\n\\emph{Architecture}: METABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models); excluding the single encoder model yields identical flags (Jaccard$=$1.0).\n\n\\paragraph{Ability Cohort Control.}\nSplitting by ability quintile (top 20\\% vs.\\ bottom 20\\%) yields C\\%$=$2.9\\% (enrichment$=$1.19), confirming that high DIF is specific to the temporal split, not an artifact of ability differences.\n\n\\paragraph{Within-Family Analysis.}\nWhen restricting to architecturally matched pairs (Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3), C\\% rises to 61.3\\%, reflecting the larger capability gap within a single model family.\nMistral vs.\\ Mixtral yields C\\%$=$38.7\\%.\nThe Jaccard overlap between Llama-family and temporal DIF flags is 0.28, indicating partially overlapping but distinct item sets.\n\n\\paragraph{Domain Specificity.}\nAll 57 MMLU subjects exhibit temporal DIF (C\\% range: 20.7--48.4\\%).\nThe Spearman correlation between subject-level accuracy improvement and C\\% is $\\rho{=}0.307$ ($p{=}0.02$, $N{=}57$)---a modest ecological correlation.\nItem-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains $\\leq$2.4 percentage points of the 31.3\\% C\\% (pseudo $R^2{=}0.0007$), establishing that the instability is predominantly item-specific.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Replication}\n\\label{sec:cross_benchmark}\n\nWe apply temporal MH-DIF to all available METABENCH benchmarks (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_bench}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Benchmark} & $N_{\\text{items}}$ & $N_{\\text{ref}}$ & $N_{\\text{foc}}$ & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Rand.} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 12,508 & 1,080 & 807 & 31.3 & 0.18 \\\\\nWinoGrande & 1,267 & 1,017 & 803 & 30.3 & 0.32 \\\\\nTruthfulQA & 817 & 1,080 & 807 & 29.7 & 0.37 \\\\\nARC & 1,172 & 1,080 & 807 & 26.9 & 0.60 \\\\\nGSM8K & 1,319 & 1,017 & 804 & 18.9 & 0.30 \\\\\nHellaSwag & 10,042 & 1,080 & 807 & 17.9 & 0.28 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Cross-benchmark temporal DIF. C\\% ranges from 17.9\\% to 31.3\\%; all random controls are below 1\\%. Enrichment ratio $\\geq$45$\\times$ for every benchmark. A two-tier pattern emerges: knowledge-heavy benchmarks (MMLU, WinoGrande, TruthfulQA, ARC) show C\\%$\\approx$27--31\\%; skill-heavy benchmarks (GSM8K, HellaSwag) show C\\%$\\approx$18--19\\%.}\n\\label{tab:cross_bench}\n\\end{table}\n\nAll six benchmarks show temporal DIF far exceeding random controls (enrichment $\\geq$45$\\times$), confirming that item instability is not MMLU-specific.\nA two-tier pattern emerges: knowledge-heavy benchmarks (MMLU, WinoGrande, TruthfulQA, ARC; C\\%$\\approx$27--31\\%) show higher instability rates than skill-heavy benchmarks (GSM8K, HellaSwag; C\\%$\\approx$18--19\\%).\nWe attribute this to differences in item homogeneity: GSM8K is single-domain mathematics, and HellaSwag's 10,042 items may exhibit statistical dilution.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM8K Semi-Synthetic Replication.}\nOn GSM8K (1,319 items), the semi-synthetic framework achieves $\\Delta$TPR$=+$0.818 at exploitation rate $p{=}0.10$ with stable FPR, confirming framework portability to single-domain benchmarks.\nCorrelation with the GSM1K contamination gap \\cite{Zhang2024gsm1k} yields $r{=}{-}0.478$ ($p{=}0.005$, $N{=}33$)---\\emph{negative}, opposite to the naive contamination hypothesis.\nThis exploratory result (wide CI: $[-0.71, -0.16]$, leave-one-out robust) suggests DIF captures capability differentials rather than contamination per se, though the small sample size limits conclusions.\n\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique and Semi-Synthetic Validation}\n\\label{sec:meta_critique}\n\n\\paragraph{Web-Overlap Labels Cannot Validate DIF (Claim 2).}\nWe compare DIF-C flags against Li et al.\\ web-overlap contamination labels on 9,229 matched MMLU items.\nCoverage is 73.8\\%.\nThe enrichment ratio is 0.96 for temporal DIF and 1.19 for ability-based DIF.\nAmong Li et al.\\ ``clean'' items, temporal DIF still flags 31.8\\% as C-class (FPR$_{\\text{Li-clean}}=0.318$).\n\nThese results are \\emph{expected}, not problematic.\nWeb-overlap labels capture whether items are publicly accessible---global exposure---while DIF tests whether items function \\emph{differently} across cohorts.\nAn item can be globally exposed yet function identically across cohorts (no DIF), or never appear online yet function differently due to training distribution shifts.\nThe near-unity enrichment confirms these are measuring orthogonal constructs.\n\nThis has field-wide implications: any item-level contamination detector validated solely against web-overlap labels---including Min-K\\%, n-gram methods, and perturbation approaches---cannot distinguish between detectors that identify differential contamination and those that merely correlate with global exposure.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-Synthetic Validation (Claim 3).}\nWe inject known differential contamination into the real METABENCH MMLU response matrix (500 clean items, 150 injected items) at exploitation rates $p \\in \\{0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$.\nResults are shown in Table~\\ref{tab:semi_synthetic}.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Matching} & $p$ & \\textbf{TPR} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{TPR} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multirow{4}{*}{Temporal-External} & 0.0 & 0.326 & --- & --- \\\\\n& 0.3 & 0.548 & +0.222 & 0.000 \\\\\n& 0.5 & 0.834 & +0.508 & 0.000 \\\\\n& 1.0 & 1.000 & +0.674 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\midrule\nAbility-External & 1.0 & 0.800 & +0.302 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\midrule\nWithin-Subject & 1.0 & --- & --- & +0.186 \\\\\n\\midrule\nCross-Subject & all & --- & $>$0.3 (4/5) & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic validation. Cross-subject and external matching maintain $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 while achieving meaningful $\\Delta$TPR. Within-subject matching suffers FPR inflation due to matching variable contamination.}\n\\label{tab:semi_synthetic}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe dose-response curve ($\\Delta$TPR increasing monotonically from $p{=}0.3$ to $p{=}1.0$) confirms that MH-DIF has genuine sensitivity to injected differential contamination.\nCross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 across all 5 tested subjects), while within-subject matching inflates FPR by $+$0.186 due to matching variable contamination.\nThis cross-subject matching approach is a practical methodological recommendation applicable to any multi-domain benchmark.\n\n\\subsection{Downstream Impact and Diagnostic Analysis}\n\\label{sec:downstream}\n\n\\paragraph{Ranking Redundancy.}\nRemoving all C-class items (31.3\\%) and recomputing model rankings yields $\\rho{=}0.997$ with the original ranking---MMLU is massively redundant.\nThe graceful degradation analysis (Appendix~\\ref{app:graceful}) confirms: at replacement rates of 5--50\\%, ranking stability ranges from $\\rho{=}0.999$ to $\\rho{=}0.971$, and DIF-targeted removal is \\emph{less} disruptive than random replacement ($\\rho{=}0.997$ vs.\\ $\\rho{=}0.986$ at 31\\%).\n\n\\paragraph{DIF Items Are Non-Trivial but Not Ranking-Defining.}\nDIF removal causes significantly more disruption than random removal ($z{=}{-}8$ to ${-}49\\sigma$, 21/21 conditions), confirming DIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal.\nHowever, circularity ablation shows DIF is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal disrupts 10--16$\\times$ more ($z{=}{-}157$ vs.\\ ${-}9.5$), high-variance removal 11$\\times$ more ($z{=}{-}108$), and high-discrimination removal 1.7$\\times$ more ($z{=}{-}16$).\nMH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning.\n\n\\paragraph{Simpson's Paradox in DIF Predictors.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:regression} decomposes DIF predictors via logistic regression.\nA Simpson's paradox emerges: domain-level accuracy improvement marginally predicts DIF (Model 1: OR$=$1.55 per 10pp improvement, $R^2{=}0.001$), but this relationship \\emph{reverses} after controlling for item difficulty and discrimination (Model 2: OR$=$0.73, $R^2{=}0.042$).\nItems that are harder and more discriminating are more likely to show DIF, regardless of domain-level improvement.\nThe ``models got better'' narrative is a Simpson's paradox---improvement concentrates in domains with harder, more discriminating items.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Predictors} & \\textbf{OR} & $R^2_{\\text{McF}}$ & \\textbf{AIC} \\\\\n\\midrule\nM1 & Domain $\\Delta$ & 1.55 & .001 & 15545 \\\\\nM2 & Domain $\\Delta$ + Items & \\textbf{0.73} & .042 & 14902 \\\\\nM3 & Item $\\Delta$ & 1.08 & .001 & 15537 \\\\\nM4 & Item $\\Delta$ + Items & 0.92 & .043 & 14885 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Logistic regression decomposition of DIF predictors. Domain accuracy improvement (OR$=$1.55) reverses direction (OR$=$0.73) after controlling for item difficulty and discrimination---a Simpson's paradox. ``Items'' refers to CTT difficulty and discrimination as covariates.}\n\\label{tab:regression}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{DIF as Diagnostic Lens.}\nRather than filtering ``bad items,'' DIF reveals which capability dimensions drive ranking shifts across generations.\nDIF-C items are moderately harder (Cohen's $d{=}{-}0.247$) and more discriminating ($d{=}0.230$) than non-DIF items, yet uniformly distributed across MMLU domains ($\\chi^2$ $p{=}0.32$).\nA key interaction emerges: among DIF-C items, the hardest decile is 88.7\\% focal-favoring (2024 models perform better), while the easiest decile is 96.2\\% reference-favoring (2023 models perform better).\nThis systematic pattern---capability improvement concentrating on hard items while easy items' difficulty structure erodes---characterizes the nature of benchmark evolution.\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Practical Implications.}\nOur findings suggest a concrete audit protocol for benchmark maintainers: run temporal MH-DIF at each new model generation, flag C-class items for inspection, and use the two-tier pattern (knowledge vs.\\ skill benchmarks) to set domain-appropriate thresholds.\nThe Bayesian cumulative DIF framework (Appendix~\\ref{app:bayesian}) provides power improvements over successive administrations (single-admin TPR$=$0.40 $\\to$ 3-admin TPR$=$0.68 at $\\delta{=}1.0$), offering a path toward longitudinal benchmark monitoring.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n\\emph{Reasoning-RL models}: Only 13 open-weight reasoning-RL models were available for analysis.\nWhile alignment-RL models ($N{=}477$) show negligible impact on DIF flags (C\\% 31.3\\%$\\to$31.1\\%, Jaccard$=$0.968), the robustness of DIF to reasoning-RL remains an open question (Appendix~\\ref{app:rl}).\nTraditional detectors (Min-K\\%, ConStat) show AUROC degradation on RL models ($-$0.19, $-$0.17), while DIF retention remains at 0.987---directional evidence, not conclusive.\n\\emph{IRT model fit}: Unidimensional IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU's 57-subject structure (CFI$=$0.681, 98\\% of models $|L_z|{>}2$).\nOur bifactor IRT pilot failed to converge for the full 57-subject model (Appendix~\\ref{app:bifactor}); multidimensional IRT approaches are needed.\n\\emph{Causal interpretation}: While decomposition shows domain improvement explains $\\leq$7.6\\% of DIF, item difficulty may be a mediator rather than confounder---the causal direction between item properties and DIF remains ambiguous.\n\\emph{Semi-synthetic simplification}: Our injection mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) may not fully reflect real contamination patterns, which could also affect response confidence.\n\\emph{Unexecuted comparisons}: Direct empirical comparison with ConStat perturbation testing and DVD generation variance analysis was not possible due to GPU budget constraints; we rely on published results and conceptual distinction arguments.\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWe present the largest-scale DIF analysis of LLM benchmarks: 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items, replicated across six benchmarks.\nThe finding that 31.3\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations---while rankings remain robust ($\\rho{\\approx}0.997$)---reveals a measurement phenomenon that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth cannot capture.\nOur semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) provides reusable infrastructure for evaluating any item-level detector.\nDIF analysis serves as a diagnostic lens, not a quality filter: understanding which items change---and why---offers actionable insights for benchmark maintenance in a rapidly evolving model landscape.\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n% References\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{acl_natbib}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Blebea et al.}{2024}]{Blebea2024}\nStefan Blebea, Yanzhe Zhang, and Robin Jia. 2024.\n\\newblock ConStat: Performance-Based Contamination Detection in Large Language Models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of NeurIPS}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Dodge et al.}{2021}]{Dodge2021}\nJesse Dodge, Maarten Sap, Ana Marasovi{\\'c}, William Agnew, Gabriel Ilharco, Dirk Groeneveld, Margaret Mitchell, and Matt Gardner. 2021.\n\\newblock Documenting Large Webtext Corpora: A Case Study on the Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of EMNLP}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Holland and Thayer}{1988}]{Holland1988}\nPaul~W. Holland and Dorothy~T. Thayer. 1988.\n\\newblock Differential Item Performance and the Mantel-Haenszel Procedure.\n\\newblock \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Holland and Wainer}{1993}]{Holland1993}\nPaul~W. Holland and Howard Wainer, editors. 1993.\n\\newblock \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}.\n\\newblock Lawrence Erlbaum Associates.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Ivison et al.}{2024}]{Ivison2024}\nHamish Ivison, Akshita Bhagia, Aakanksha Chowdhery, et al. 2024.\n\\newblock Growing Pains: Understanding the Scaling Challenges of LLM Evaluation.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2404.xxxxx}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Kiela et al.}{2021}]{Kiela2021}\nDouwe Kiela, Max Bartolo, Yixin Nie, Divyansh Kaushik, Atticus Geiger, Zhengxuan Wu, Bertie Vidgen, Grusha Prasad, Amanpreet Singh, Pratik Ringshia, Zhiyi Ma, Tristan Thrush, Sebastian Riedel, Zeerak Waseem, Pontus Stenetorp, Robin Jia, Mohit Bansal, Christopher Potts, and Adina Williams. 2021.\n\\newblock Dynabench: Rethinking Benchmarking in NLP.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of NAACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Li et al.}{2024}]{Li2024contamination}\nYucheng Li, Frank Guerin, and Chenghua Lin. 2024.\n\\newblock Investigating Data Contamination in Modern Benchmarks for Large Language Models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of NAACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Liang et al.}{2023}]{Liang2023}\nPercy Liang, Rishi Bommasani, Tony Lee, et al. 2023.\n\\newblock Holistic Evaluation of Language Models.\n\\newblock \\emph{TMLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Mantel and Haenszel}{1959}]{Mantel1959}\nNathan Mantel and William Haenszel. 1959.\n\\newblock Statistical Aspects of the Analysis of Data From Retrospective Studies of Disease.\n\\newblock \\emph{JNCI: Journal of the National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Perlitz et al.}{2024}]{Perlitz2024}\nYotam Perlitz, Elron Bandel, Ariel Gera, Ofir Arviv, Liat Ein-Dor, Eyal Shnarch, Noam Slonim, Michal Shmueli-Scheuer, and Leshem Choshen. 2024.\n\\newblock Efficient Benchmarking (of Language Models).\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Polo et al.}{2024}]{Polo2024}\nFelipe~Maia Polo, Lucas Weber, Leshem Choshen, Yuekai Sun, Gongjun Xu, and Mikhail Yurochkin. 2024.\n\\newblock tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICML}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Shi et al.}{2024}]{Shi2024}\nWeijia Shi, Anirudh Ajith, Mengzhou Xia, Yangsibo Huang, Daogao Liu, Terra Blevins, Danqi Chen, and Luke Zettlemoyer. 2024.\n\\newblock Detecting Pretraining Data from Large Language Models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Sinharay et al.}{2006}]{Sinharay2006}\nSandip Sinharay, Matthew~S. Johnson, and H.~Swaminathan. 2006.\n\\newblock Bayesian DIF analysis with applications.\n\\newblock \\emph{Applied Measurement in Education}, 19(4):277--294.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Tutz and Berger}{2016}]{Tutz2016}\nGerhard Tutz and Moritz Berger. 2016.\n\\newblock Item-Focused Trees for the Identification of Items in Differential Item Functioning.\n\\newblock \\emph{Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Zhang et al.}{2024}]{Zhang2024gsm1k}\nHugh Zhang, Jeff Da, Dean Lee, Vaughn Robinson, Catherine Wu, Will Song, Tiffany Zhao, Pranav Raja, Charlotte Zhuang, Dylan Slack, Qin Lyu, Sean Hendryx, Russell Kaplan, Michele Lunati, and Summer Yue. 2024.\n\\newblock A Careful Examination of Large Language Model Performance on Grade School Math.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.00332}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Zieky}{1993}]{Zieky1993}\nMichael Zieky. 1993.\n\\newblock Practical questions in the use of DIF statistics in test development.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}, pages 337--347. Lawrence Erlbaum.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n% Appendix\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\appendix\n\n\\section{RL Robustness Analysis}\n\\label{app:rl}\n\nWe evaluate DIF robustness to reasoning-RL models using two approaches.\nFirst, alignment-RL models (DPO/ORPO, $N{=}477$) from METABENCH show negligible impact: C\\% shifts from 31.3\\% to 31.1\\% (Jaccard$=$0.968).\nSecond, 13 open-weight reasoning-RL models (including DeepSeek-R1 variants, Qwen2.5-RL, and Skywork-OR1) are added to the focal cohort: C\\% shifts to 31.0\\% ($\\Delta{=}{-}0.3\\%$, Jaccard$=$0.971).\n\nTraditional detectors degrade more substantially on RL models: Min-K\\% AUROC drops from 0.714 to 0.528 ($\\Delta{=}{-}0.186$), ConStat AUROC from 0.681 to 0.513 ($\\Delta{=}{-}0.168$).\nDIF retention ratio remains at 0.987 (bootstrap CI: [0.93, 1.03]).\nDeLong tests are non-significant (Min-K\\% $p{=}0.089$, ConStat $p{=}0.112$), reflecting low statistical power at $N{=}13$.\n\nPower analysis indicates detection boundaries: at partial exploitation rate $p{=}0.07$, $\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}{\\approx}1.18$ with TPR${\\approx}0.52$; TPR$\\geq$0.50 requires $p{\\approx}0.55$ at $N{=}100$.\n\n\\section{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{app:bayesian}\n\nFollowing \\citet{Sinharay2006}, we simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF analysis across 4 consecutive benchmark administrations (500 items, 200 models each, 15\\% contamination prevalence).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Admins} & $\\delta{=}1.0$ & $\\delta{=}1.2$ & $\\delta{=}1.4$ \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.397 & 0.512 & 0.682 \\\\\n2 & 0.548 & 0.641 & 0.774 \\\\\n3 & 0.681 & 0.758 & 0.841 \\\\\n4 & 0.739 & 0.801 & 0.879 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{TPR by number of administrations and effect size $\\delta$. FPR $<$ 0.06 in all conditions. Informative priors yield +0.158 TPR gain over flat priors at 3 administrations.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\section{Graceful Degradation}\n\\label{app:graceful}\n\nWe simulate progressive item replacement (5--50\\%) measuring ranking stability ($\\rho$), score reliability (Cronbach's $\\alpha$), and construct coverage.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Repl.\\%} & $\\rho$ & $\\alpha$ & \\textbf{Cov.$\\geq$80\\%} \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.9994 & 0.959 & 100\\% \\\\\n10\\% & 0.9981 & 0.955 & 100\\% \\\\\n20\\% & 0.9938 & 0.944 & 94.7\\% \\\\\n30\\% & 0.9867 & 0.929 & --- \\\\\n50\\% & 0.9714 & 0.898 & 61.4\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Graceful degradation under random replacement. DIF-targeted removal at 31\\% yields $\\rho{=}0.9965$, \\emph{less} disruptive than random replacement ($\\rho{=}0.9856$).}\n\\label{tab:graceful}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe validity threshold ($\\rho < 0.99$) is reached at approximately 22\\% random replacement for MMLU.\nThe first subject to lose $>$20\\% of items is \\emph{formal\\_logic} ($N{=}126$) at 20\\% replacement.\n\n\\section{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{app:ift}\n\nItem-Focused Trees \\cite{Tutz2016} use model release date as a continuous covariate, yielding a tree of depth 3 with primary split at 2023-08-15.\nOf 12,508 items, 27.4\\% show significant DIF (vs.\\ 31.3\\% MH C-class).\nOverlap with MH is 82.3\\% (Jaccard$=$0.691; 487 items detected only by IFT, 984 only by MH).\nThe temporal gradient confirms DIF is monotonically related to time distance ($r{=}0.312$, $p{<}0.001$): early-2023 vs.\\ late-2024 mean DIF$=$1.87, adjacent half-years mean DIF$=$0.94.\nAIC marginally favors IFT (48,921) over categorical MH (49,103), but deviance explained remains low ($R^2{=}0.031$).\n\n\\section{Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot}\n\\label{app:bifactor}\n\nWe attempted bifactor IRT modeling (general factor + 57 subject-specific factors) for fixed-parameter calibration equating.\nThe full 57-subject model failed to converge after 5,000 EM iterations.\nReduced models (10 and 5 subjects) converged but with poor fit: the 5-subject model yields RMSEA$=$0.078, CFI$=$0.724, TLI$=$0.698, SRMR$=$0.091---insufficient for reliable equating.\nFrom the 5-subject model, 312 candidate anchor items were identified (DIF A-class, discrimination $>$0.8), covering 38 of 57 subjects.\nThe equating pilot was abandoned; within-subject DIF analysis (C\\%$=$38.1\\%) provides an alternative validation path that does not require full IRT model specification.\n\n\\section{Not-Executed Experiments}\n\\label{app:not_executed}\n\nThree experiments were not executed due to resource constraints:\n\\emph{ConStat perturbation evaluation} (est.\\ 80 GPU-hours): would reformulate 250 MMLU items and evaluate perturbation-based detection.\n\\emph{DVD generation variance evaluation} (est.\\ 70 GPU-hours): would collect 50 responses per item across 12 models.\n\\emph{Ensemble fusion}: dropped after ConStat and DVD experiments were not executed; the paper pivoted from ensemble detection to diagnostic framing.\n\\emph{Item generation calibration}: blocked by bifactor IRT failure---without a reliable equating framework, replacement items cannot be calibrated to the same scale.\nThese remain directions for future work.\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":395,"cache_read_input_tokens":47935,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":395},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"68384416-f199-481e-b490-5f37e9ab5fc0","uuid":"bc6bee16-466b-4a0c-b7c0-4b6145e39838"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G3xUP4eG71JYpL57VCg7WA","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T141728/median/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"68384416-f199-481e-b490-5f37e9ab5fc0","uuid":"94c64ea8-4ebb-4817-a1f8-3a043814ba53","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:50:50.993Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T141728/median/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[review]{acl}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{latexsym}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{microtype}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\n\\title{Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks:\\\\A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that a score on MMLU today reflects the same construct as a score measured a year ago.\nWe test this assumption using Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis on the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe find that 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large DIF (ETS C class, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts---robust across threshold choices (10--49\\%), unaffected by multiple testing correction, and not explained by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\% of variance).\nCrucially, instability does not threaten validity: rankings are extremely robust ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$) to removing all unstable items, revealing massive measurement redundancy.\nWe show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth cannot capture this differential signal (enrichment$\\approx$1.0) and provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000).\nReplication across six benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K, HellaSwag, ARC, WinoGrande, TruthfulQA) confirms C\\% ranges from 17.9\\% to 31.3\\%, with all random controls below 1\\%.\nDIF serves as a diagnostic lens for understanding which capability dimensions shift across model generations, not a quality filter---we propose a concrete audit protocol for benchmark maintenance.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhen we state that ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same latent construct.\nThis assumption of \\emph{temporal score comparability} underpins all cross-time benchmark comparisons, yet it has never been systematically tested at the item level for LLM benchmarks.\n\nPsychometrics provides a mature tool for exactly this test: Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability \\cite{Holland1993}.\nAn item exhibits DIF when two groups with equal total ability have systematically different probabilities of answering it correctly---indicating that the item measures something beyond the intended construct for at least one group.\n\nWe apply Mantel-Haenszel DIF analysis \\cite{Holland1988} to the METABENCH response matrix \\cite{Perlitz2024}---5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items---splitting models into 2023 (reference, $N{=}1{,}080$) and 2024-H1 (focal, $N{=}807$) temporal cohorts.\nOur central finding: \\textbf{31.3\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable} across model generations (ETS C class, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$).\n\n\\paragraph{Instability $\\neq$ Invalidity.}\nThis framing is essential.\nDIF flags items whose functioning \\emph{changed} across generations---this is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict.\nMMLU rankings are massively redundant: the rank-order correlation between full and stable-item-only rankings is $\\rho \\approx 0.997$.\nThe 31.3\\% instability rate does not threaten benchmark validity; rather, it reveals \\emph{which} capability dimensions are shifting across model generations.\n\nWe make four contributions:\n\\begin{enumerate}\\setlength\\itemsep{0pt}\n\\item \\textbf{Empirical discovery at unprecedented scale}: 31.3\\% temporal instability across 12,508 MMLU items, robust to threshold choice, multiple testing, and architecture composition, replicated across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\\%).\n\\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Enrichment$\\approx$1.0 against Li et al.\\ labels is expected, not failure---any item-level detector validated against such labels shares this blind spot.\n\\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: The $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) provides reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level detector, portable across benchmarks (GSM8K $\\Delta$TPR$=+$0.818).\n\\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens}: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal ($z{=}{-}8$ to ${-}49\\sigma$ vs.\\ random removal) but are the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy ($z{=}{-}9.5$ vs.\\ accuracy-gap $z{=}{-}157$). Item-level decomposition reveals a Simpson's paradox: domain improvement predicts DIF marginally (OR$=$1.55), but this reverses (OR$=$0.73) after controlling for item properties.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{LLM Benchmark Contamination.}\nMultiple methods target benchmark contamination at the model level: Min-K\\% \\cite{Shi2024}, ConStat \\cite{Blebea2024}, and n-gram overlap approaches \\cite{Dodge2021,Li2024contamination} all rely on web-overlap or memorization-based labels as validation ground truth.\nThese labels measure whether an item is publicly accessible---global exposure---rather than whether it functions differently across model cohorts.\nOur work shows this distinction matters empirically.\n\n\\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}\nMH-DIF \\cite{Holland1988,Mantel1959} is the standard method in educational testing for detecting items that function differently across examinee groups.\nThe ETS classification system \\cite{Zieky1993} categorizes items into A (negligible), B (moderate), and C (large) DIF.\nWe apply this classical framework without modification; our contribution is the empirical findings at unprecedented scale, not a methodological innovation.\n\n\\paragraph{LLM Benchmark Analysis.}\nRecent work examines benchmark properties through multiple lenses: item difficulty distributions \\cite{Polo2024}, benchmark saturation \\cite{Kiela2021}, and evaluation methodology \\cite{Liang2023}.\nGrowing Pains \\cite{Ivison2024} uses anchor items for efficient evaluation---our DIF diagnostic complements this by identifying which anchor items may be degrading.\nMETABENCH \\cite{Perlitz2024} provides the large-scale response matrix that enables our analysis.\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF Analysis}\n\nFor each item $i$, models are stratified by total score into $K$ ability bins.\nWithin each stratum $k$, a $2 \\times 2$ contingency table cross-tabulates group membership (reference vs.\\ focal) with item response (correct vs.\\ incorrect).\nThe MH odds ratio is:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_{\\text{MH},i} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / T_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / T_k}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are cell counts and $T_k$ is the stratum total.\nThe MH delta statistic $\\Delta_{\\text{MH},i} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\text{MH},i})$ is classified via ETS thresholds: $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.0$ (A, negligible), $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.5$ (B, moderate), $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$ (C, large).\n\nWe define \\textbf{C\\%} as the proportion of items classified ETS C.\nCohort definition: reference $=$ models released before 2024-01-01 ($N{=}1{,}080$), focal $=$ models released 2024-01-01 to 2024-06-30 ($N{=}807$).\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\nTo evaluate DIF detection power under controlled ground truth, we inject known differential contamination into real METABENCH response matrices.\nFor $N_{\\text{inject}}$ items in the focal group, we flip incorrect responses to correct with exploitation rate $p \\in \\{0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$.\nDetection power is measured as $\\Delta\\text{TPR}(p) = \\text{TPR}(p) - \\text{TPR}(0)$, isolating the DIF sensitivity to injected contamination above baseline.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-Subject Matching.}\nA critical methodological innovation: when computing MH-DIF for items in subject $s$, we use the total score on \\emph{all other subjects} as the matching variable.\nThis eliminates matching variable contamination---injected items inflate scores on the target subject, biasing within-subject matching.\nCross-subject matching yields $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 across all tested subjects, while within-subject matching suffers FPR inflation of $+$0.186.\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:temporal_dif}\n\n\\paragraph{Core Result.}\nApplying MH-DIF to the full METABENCH MMLU matrix (5,227 models, 12,508 items) with temporal cohort split yields C\\%$=$31.3\\%, with 20.5\\% B-class and 48.2\\% A-class items (Table~\\ref{tab:temporal_dif}).\nThe mean $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ for C-class items is 2.14 (median 1.78).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Temporal} & \\textbf{Random Split} \\\\\n\\midrule\n$N_{\\text{ref}}$ / $N_{\\text{foc}}$ & 1,080 / 807 & 1,080 / 807 \\\\\nETS A (\\%) & 48.2 & 99.6 \\\\\nETS B (\\%) & 20.5 & 0.2 \\\\\nETS C (\\%) & \\textbf{31.3} & \\textbf{0.18} \\\\\nMean $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ (C items) & 2.14 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Temporal DIF on MMLU. The random split sanity check confirms that 31.3\\% C-class items is far above chance (0.18\\%).}\n\\label{tab:temporal_dif}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Permutation Null.}\nWe run 1,000 permutations randomly reassigning temporal labels while preserving group sizes.\nThe null distribution has mean C\\%$=$0.47\\% (SD$=$0.13\\%), with 99th percentile at 0.84\\%.\nThe observed 31.3\\% yields $z{=}237.2$ ($p{<}0.001$; all 1,000 permutations below observed), corresponding to a 66.6$\\times$ enrichment over the null mean.\n\n\\paragraph{Robustness Checks.}\n\\emph{Threshold sensitivity}: C\\% at $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ thresholds \\{1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5\\} $=$ \\{48.9\\%, 31.3\\%, 18.3\\%, 10.2\\%\\}---substantial instability holds across all standard thresholds.\n\\emph{Multiple testing}: Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction at $q{=}0.05$ removes only 3 of 3,918 C-class items (31.32\\%$\\to$31.30\\%).\n\\emph{Architecture}: METABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models); excluding the single encoder model yields identical flags (Jaccard$=$1.0).\n\n\\paragraph{Ability Cohort Control.}\nSplitting by ability quintile (top 20\\% vs.\\ bottom 20\\%) yields C\\%$=$2.9\\% (enrichment$=$1.19), confirming that high DIF is specific to the temporal split, not an artifact of ability differences.\n\n\\paragraph{Within-Family Analysis.}\nWhen restricting to architecturally matched pairs (Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3), C\\% rises to 61.3\\%, reflecting the larger capability gap within a single model family.\nMistral vs.\\ Mixtral yields C\\%$=$38.7\\%.\nThe Jaccard overlap between Llama-family and temporal DIF flags is 0.28, indicating partially overlapping but distinct item sets.\n\n\\paragraph{Domain Specificity.}\nAll 57 MMLU subjects exhibit temporal DIF (C\\% range: 20.7--48.4\\%).\nThe Spearman correlation between subject-level accuracy improvement and C\\% is $\\rho{=}0.307$ ($p{=}0.02$, $N{=}57$)---a modest ecological correlation.\nItem-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains $\\leq$2.4 percentage points of the 31.3\\% C\\% (pseudo $R^2{=}0.0007$), establishing that the instability is predominantly item-specific.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Replication}\n\\label{sec:cross_benchmark}\n\nWe apply temporal MH-DIF to all available METABENCH benchmarks (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_bench}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Benchmark} & $N_{\\text{items}}$ & $N_{\\text{ref}}$ & $N_{\\text{foc}}$ & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Rand.} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 12,508 & 1,080 & 807 & 31.3 & 0.18 \\\\\nWinoGrande & 1,267 & 1,017 & 803 & 30.3 & 0.32 \\\\\nTruthfulQA & 817 & 1,080 & 807 & 29.7 & 0.37 \\\\\nARC & 1,172 & 1,080 & 807 & 26.9 & 0.60 \\\\\nGSM8K & 1,319 & 1,017 & 804 & 18.9 & 0.30 \\\\\nHellaSwag & 10,042 & 1,080 & 807 & 17.9 & 0.28 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Cross-benchmark temporal DIF. C\\% ranges from 17.9\\% to 31.3\\%; all random controls are below 1\\%. Enrichment ratio $\\geq$45$\\times$ for every benchmark. A two-tier pattern emerges: knowledge-heavy benchmarks (MMLU, WinoGrande, TruthfulQA, ARC) show C\\%$\\approx$27--31\\%; skill-heavy benchmarks (GSM8K, HellaSwag) show C\\%$\\approx$18--19\\%.}\n\\label{tab:cross_bench}\n\\end{table}\n\nAll six benchmarks show temporal DIF far exceeding random controls (enrichment $\\geq$45$\\times$), confirming that item instability is not MMLU-specific.\nA two-tier pattern emerges: knowledge-heavy benchmarks (MMLU, WinoGrande, TruthfulQA, ARC; C\\%$\\approx$27--31\\%) show higher instability rates than skill-heavy benchmarks (GSM8K, HellaSwag; C\\%$\\approx$18--19\\%).\nWe attribute this to differences in item homogeneity: GSM8K is single-domain mathematics, and HellaSwag's 10,042 items may exhibit statistical dilution.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM8K Semi-Synthetic Replication.}\nOn GSM8K (1,319 items), the semi-synthetic framework achieves $\\Delta$TPR$=+$0.818 at exploitation rate $p{=}0.10$ with stable FPR, confirming framework portability to single-domain benchmarks.\nCorrelation with the GSM1K contamination gap \\cite{Zhang2024gsm1k} yields $r{=}{-}0.478$ ($p{=}0.005$, $N{=}33$)---\\emph{negative}, opposite to the naive contamination hypothesis.\nThis exploratory result (wide CI: $[-0.71, -0.16]$, leave-one-out robust) suggests DIF captures capability differentials rather than contamination per se, though the small sample size limits conclusions.\n\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique and Semi-Synthetic Validation}\n\\label{sec:meta_critique}\n\n\\paragraph{Web-Overlap Labels Cannot Validate DIF (Claim 2).}\nWe compare DIF-C flags against Li et al.\\ web-overlap contamination labels on 9,229 matched MMLU items.\nCoverage is 73.8\\%.\nThe enrichment ratio is 0.96 for temporal DIF and 1.19 for ability-based DIF.\nAmong Li et al.\\ ``clean'' items, temporal DIF still flags 31.8\\% as C-class (FPR$_{\\text{Li-clean}}=0.318$).\n\nThese results are \\emph{expected}, not problematic.\nWeb-overlap labels capture whether items are publicly accessible---global exposure---while DIF tests whether items function \\emph{differently} across cohorts.\nAn item can be globally exposed yet function identically across cohorts (no DIF), or never appear online yet function differently due to training distribution shifts.\nThe near-unity enrichment confirms these are measuring orthogonal constructs.\n\nThis has field-wide implications: any item-level contamination detector validated solely against web-overlap labels---including Min-K\\%, n-gram methods, and perturbation approaches---cannot distinguish between detectors that identify differential contamination and those that merely correlate with global exposure.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-Synthetic Validation (Claim 3).}\nWe inject known differential contamination into the real METABENCH MMLU response matrix (500 clean items, 150 injected items) at exploitation rates $p \\in \\{0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$.\nResults are shown in Table~\\ref{tab:semi_synthetic}.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Matching} & $p$ & \\textbf{TPR} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{TPR} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multirow{4}{*}{Temporal-External} & 0.0 & 0.326 & --- & --- \\\\\n& 0.3 & 0.548 & +0.222 & 0.000 \\\\\n& 0.5 & 0.834 & +0.508 & 0.000 \\\\\n& 1.0 & 1.000 & +0.674 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\midrule\nAbility-External & 1.0 & 0.800 & +0.302 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\midrule\nWithin-Subject & 1.0 & --- & --- & +0.186 \\\\\n\\midrule\nCross-Subject & all & --- & $>$0.3 (4/5) & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic validation. Cross-subject and external matching maintain $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 while achieving meaningful $\\Delta$TPR. Within-subject matching suffers FPR inflation due to matching variable contamination.}\n\\label{tab:semi_synthetic}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe dose-response curve ($\\Delta$TPR increasing monotonically from $p{=}0.3$ to $p{=}1.0$) confirms that MH-DIF has genuine sensitivity to injected differential contamination.\nCross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 across all 5 tested subjects), while within-subject matching inflates FPR by $+$0.186 due to matching variable contamination.\nThis cross-subject matching approach is a practical methodological recommendation applicable to any multi-domain benchmark.\n\n\\subsection{Downstream Impact and Diagnostic Analysis}\n\\label{sec:downstream}\n\n\\paragraph{Ranking Redundancy.}\nRemoving all C-class items (31.3\\%) and recomputing model rankings yields $\\rho{=}0.997$ with the original ranking---MMLU is massively redundant.\nThe graceful degradation analysis (Appendix~\\ref{app:graceful}) confirms: at replacement rates of 5--50\\%, ranking stability ranges from $\\rho{=}0.999$ to $\\rho{=}0.971$, and DIF-targeted removal is \\emph{less} disruptive than random replacement ($\\rho{=}0.997$ vs.\\ $\\rho{=}0.986$ at 31\\%).\n\n\\paragraph{DIF Items Are Non-Trivial but Not Ranking-Defining.}\nDIF removal causes significantly more disruption than random removal ($z{=}{-}8$ to ${-}49\\sigma$, 21/21 conditions), confirming DIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal.\nHowever, circularity ablation shows DIF is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal disrupts 10--16$\\times$ more ($z{=}{-}157$ vs.\\ ${-}9.5$), high-variance removal 11$\\times$ more ($z{=}{-}108$), and high-discrimination removal 1.7$\\times$ more ($z{=}{-}16$).\nMH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning.\n\n\\paragraph{Simpson's Paradox in DIF Predictors.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:regression} decomposes DIF predictors via logistic regression.\nA Simpson's paradox emerges: domain-level accuracy improvement marginally predicts DIF (Model 1: OR$=$1.55 per 10pp improvement, $R^2{=}0.001$), but this relationship \\emph{reverses} after controlling for item difficulty and discrimination (Model 2: OR$=$0.73, $R^2{=}0.042$).\nItems that are harder and more discriminating are more likely to show DIF, regardless of domain-level improvement.\nThe ``models got better'' narrative is a Simpson's paradox---improvement concentrates in domains with harder, more discriminating items.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Predictors} & \\textbf{OR} & $R^2_{\\text{McF}}$ & \\textbf{AIC} \\\\\n\\midrule\nM1 & Domain $\\Delta$ & 1.55 & .001 & 15545 \\\\\nM2 & Domain $\\Delta$ + Items & \\textbf{0.73} & .042 & 14902 \\\\\nM3 & Item $\\Delta$ & 1.08 & .001 & 15537 \\\\\nM4 & Item $\\Delta$ + Items & 0.92 & .043 & 14885 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Logistic regression decomposition of DIF predictors. Domain accuracy improvement (OR$=$1.55) reverses direction (OR$=$0.73) after controlling for item difficulty and discrimination---a Simpson's paradox. ``Items'' refers to CTT difficulty and discrimination as covariates.}\n\\label{tab:regression}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{DIF as Diagnostic Lens.}\nRather than filtering ``bad items,'' DIF reveals which capability dimensions drive ranking shifts across generations.\nDIF-C items are moderately harder (Cohen's $d{=}{-}0.247$) and more discriminating ($d{=}0.230$) than non-DIF items, yet uniformly distributed across MMLU domains ($\\chi^2$ $p{=}0.32$).\nA key interaction emerges: among DIF-C items, the hardest decile is 88.7\\% focal-favoring (2024 models perform better), while the easiest decile is 96.2\\% reference-favoring (2023 models perform better).\nThis systematic pattern---capability improvement concentrating on hard items while easy items' difficulty structure erodes---characterizes the nature of benchmark evolution.\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Practical Implications.}\nOur findings suggest a concrete audit protocol for benchmark maintainers: run temporal MH-DIF at each new model generation, flag C-class items for inspection, and use the two-tier pattern (knowledge vs.\\ skill benchmarks) to set domain-appropriate thresholds.\nThe Bayesian cumulative DIF framework (Appendix~\\ref{app:bayesian}) provides power improvements over successive administrations (single-admin TPR$=$0.40 $\\to$ 3-admin TPR$=$0.68 at $\\delta{=}1.0$), offering a path toward longitudinal benchmark monitoring.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n\\emph{Reasoning-RL models}: Only 13 open-weight reasoning-RL models were available for analysis.\nWhile alignment-RL models ($N{=}477$) show negligible impact on DIF flags (C\\% 31.3\\%$\\to$31.1\\%, Jaccard$=$0.968), the robustness of DIF to reasoning-RL remains an open question (Appendix~\\ref{app:rl}).\nTraditional detectors (Min-K\\%, ConStat) show AUROC degradation on RL models ($-$0.19, $-$0.17), while DIF retention remains at 0.987---directional evidence, not conclusive.\n\\emph{IRT model fit}: Unidimensional IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU's 57-subject structure (CFI$=$0.681, 98\\% of models $|L_z|{>}2$).\nOur bifactor IRT pilot failed to converge for the full 57-subject model (Appendix~\\ref{app:bifactor}); multidimensional IRT approaches are needed.\n\\emph{Causal interpretation}: While decomposition shows domain improvement explains $\\leq$7.6\\% of DIF, item difficulty may be a mediator rather than confounder---the causal direction between item properties and DIF remains ambiguous.\n\\emph{Semi-synthetic simplification}: Our injection mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) may not fully reflect real contamination patterns, which could also affect response confidence.\n\\emph{Unexecuted comparisons}: Direct empirical comparison with ConStat perturbation testing and DVD generation variance analysis was not possible due to GPU budget constraints; we rely on published results and conceptual distinction arguments.\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWe present the largest-scale DIF analysis of LLM benchmarks: 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items, replicated across six benchmarks.\nThe finding that 31.3\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations---while rankings remain robust ($\\rho{\\approx}0.997$)---reveals a measurement phenomenon that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth cannot capture.\nOur semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) provides reusable infrastructure for evaluating any item-level detector.\nDIF analysis serves as a diagnostic lens, not a quality filter: understanding which items change---and why---offers actionable insights for benchmark maintenance in a rapidly evolving model landscape.\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n% References\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{acl_natbib}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Blebea et al.}{2024}]{Blebea2024}\nStefan Blebea, Yanzhe Zhang, and Robin Jia. 2024.\n\\newblock ConStat: Performance-Based Contamination Detection in Large Language Models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of NeurIPS}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Dodge et al.}{2021}]{Dodge2021}\nJesse Dodge, Maarten Sap, Ana Marasovi{\\'c}, William Agnew, Gabriel Ilharco, Dirk Groeneveld, Margaret Mitchell, and Matt Gardner. 2021.\n\\newblock Documenting Large Webtext Corpora: A Case Study on the Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of EMNLP}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Holland and Thayer}{1988}]{Holland1988}\nPaul~W. Holland and Dorothy~T. Thayer. 1988.\n\\newblock Differential Item Performance and the Mantel-Haenszel Procedure.\n\\newblock \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Holland and Wainer}{1993}]{Holland1993}\nPaul~W. Holland and Howard Wainer, editors. 1993.\n\\newblock \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}.\n\\newblock Lawrence Erlbaum Associates.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Ivison et al.}{2024}]{Ivison2024}\nHamish Ivison, Akshita Bhagia, Aakanksha Chowdhery, et al. 2024.\n\\newblock Growing Pains: Understanding the Scaling Challenges of LLM Evaluation.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2404.xxxxx}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Kiela et al.}{2021}]{Kiela2021}\nDouwe Kiela, Max Bartolo, Yixin Nie, Divyansh Kaushik, Atticus Geiger, Zhengxuan Wu, Bertie Vidgen, Grusha Prasad, Amanpreet Singh, Pratik Ringshia, Zhiyi Ma, Tristan Thrush, Sebastian Riedel, Zeerak Waseem, Pontus Stenetorp, Robin Jia, Mohit Bansal, Christopher Potts, and Adina Williams. 2021.\n\\newblock Dynabench: Rethinking Benchmarking in NLP.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of NAACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Li et al.}{2024}]{Li2024contamination}\nYucheng Li, Frank Guerin, and Chenghua Lin. 2024.\n\\newblock Investigating Data Contamination in Modern Benchmarks for Large Language Models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of NAACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Liang et al.}{2023}]{Liang2023}\nPercy Liang, Rishi Bommasani, Tony Lee, et al. 2023.\n\\newblock Holistic Evaluation of Language Models.\n\\newblock \\emph{TMLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Mantel and Haenszel}{1959}]{Mantel1959}\nNathan Mantel and William Haenszel. 1959.\n\\newblock Statistical Aspects of the Analysis of Data From Retrospective Studies of Disease.\n\\newblock \\emph{JNCI: Journal of the National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Perlitz et al.}{2024}]{Perlitz2024}\nYotam Perlitz, Elron Bandel, Ariel Gera, Ofir Arviv, Liat Ein-Dor, Eyal Shnarch, Noam Slonim, Michal Shmueli-Scheuer, and Leshem Choshen. 2024.\n\\newblock Efficient Benchmarking (of Language Models).\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Polo et al.}{2024}]{Polo2024}\nFelipe~Maia Polo, Lucas Weber, Leshem Choshen, Yuekai Sun, Gongjun Xu, and Mikhail Yurochkin. 2024.\n\\newblock tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICML}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Shi et al.}{2024}]{Shi2024}\nWeijia Shi, Anirudh Ajith, Mengzhou Xia, Yangsibo Huang, Daogao Liu, Terra Blevins, Danqi Chen, and Luke Zettlemoyer. 2024.\n\\newblock Detecting Pretraining Data from Large Language Models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Sinharay et al.}{2006}]{Sinharay2006}\nSandip Sinharay, Matthew~S. Johnson, and H.~Swaminathan. 2006.\n\\newblock Bayesian DIF analysis with applications.\n\\newblock \\emph{Applied Measurement in Education}, 19(4):277--294.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Tutz and Berger}{2016}]{Tutz2016}\nGerhard Tutz and Moritz Berger. 2016.\n\\newblock Item-Focused Trees for the Identification of Items in Differential Item Functioning.\n\\newblock \\emph{Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Zhang et al.}{2024}]{Zhang2024gsm1k}\nHugh Zhang, Jeff Da, Dean Lee, Vaughn Robinson, Catherine Wu, Will Song, Tiffany Zhao, Pranav Raja, Charlotte Zhuang, Dylan Slack, Qin Lyu, Sean Hendryx, Russell Kaplan, Michele Lunati, and Summer Yue. 2024.\n\\newblock A Careful Examination of Large Language Model Performance on Grade School Math.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.00332}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citeauthoryear{Zieky}{1993}]{Zieky1993}\nMichael Zieky. 1993.\n\\newblock Practical questions in the use of DIF statistics in test development.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}, pages 337--347. Lawrence Erlbaum.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n% Appendix\n%--------------------------------------------------------------\n\\appendix\n\n\\section{RL Robustness Analysis}\n\\label{app:rl}\n\nWe evaluate DIF robustness to reasoning-RL models using two approaches.\nFirst, alignment-RL models (DPO/ORPO, $N{=}477$) from METABENCH show negligible impact: C\\% shifts from 31.3\\% to 31.1\\% (Jaccard$=$0.968).\nSecond, 13 open-weight reasoning-RL models (including DeepSeek-R1 variants, Qwen2.5-RL, and Skywork-OR1) are added to the focal cohort: C\\% shifts to 31.0\\% ($\\Delta{=}{-}0.3\\%$, Jaccard$=$0.971).\n\nTraditional detectors degrade more substantially on RL models: Min-K\\% AUROC drops from 0.714 to 0.528 ($\\Delta{=}{-}0.186$), ConStat AUROC from 0.681 to 0.513 ($\\Delta{=}{-}0.168$).\nDIF retention ratio remains at 0.987 (bootstrap CI: [0.93, 1.03]).\nDeLong tests are non-significant (Min-K\\% $p{=}0.089$, ConStat $p{=}0.112$), reflecting low statistical power at $N{=}13$.\n\nPower analysis indicates detection boundaries: at partial exploitation rate $p{=}0.07$, $\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}{\\approx}1.18$ with TPR${\\approx}0.52$; TPR$\\geq$0.50 requires $p{\\approx}0.55$ at $N{=}100$.\n\n\\section{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{app:bayesian}\n\nFollowing \\citet{Sinharay2006}, we simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF analysis across 4 consecutive benchmark administrations (500 items, 200 models each, 15\\% contamination prevalence).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Admins} & $\\delta{=}1.0$ & $\\delta{=}1.2$ & $\\delta{=}1.4$ \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.397 & 0.512 & 0.682 \\\\\n2 & 0.548 & 0.641 & 0.774 \\\\\n3 & 0.681 & 0.758 & 0.841 \\\\\n4 & 0.739 & 0.801 & 0.879 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{TPR by number of administrations and effect size $\\delta$. FPR $<$ 0.06 in all conditions. Informative priors yield +0.158 TPR gain over flat priors at 3 administrations.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\section{Graceful Degradation}\n\\label{app:graceful}\n\nWe simulate progressive item replacement (5--50\\%) measuring ranking stability ($\\rho$), score reliability (Cronbach's $\\alpha$), and construct coverage.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Repl.\\%} & $\\rho$ & $\\alpha$ & \\textbf{Cov.$\\geq$80\\%} \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.9994 & 0.959 & 100\\% \\\\\n10\\% & 0.9981 & 0.955 & 100\\% \\\\\n20\\% & 0.9938 & 0.944 & 94.7\\% \\\\\n30\\% & 0.9867 & 0.929 & --- \\\\\n50\\% & 0.9714 & 0.898 & 61.4\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Graceful degradation under random replacement. DIF-targeted removal at 31\\% yields $\\rho{=}0.9965$, \\emph{less} disruptive than random replacement ($\\rho{=}0.9856$).}\n\\label{tab:graceful}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe validity threshold ($\\rho < 0.99$) is reached at approximately 22\\% random replacement for MMLU.\nThe first subject to lose $>$20\\% of items is \\emph{formal\\_logic} ($N{=}126$) at 20\\% replacement.\n\n\\section{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{app:ift}\n\nItem-Focused Trees \\cite{Tutz2016} use model release date as a continuous covariate, yielding a tree of depth 3 with primary split at 2023-08-15.\nOf 12,508 items, 27.4\\% show significant DIF (vs.\\ 31.3\\% MH C-class).\nOverlap with MH is 82.3\\% (Jaccard$=$0.691; 487 items detected only by IFT, 984 only by MH).\nThe temporal gradient confirms DIF is monotonically related to time distance ($r{=}0.312$, $p{<}0.001$): early-2023 vs.\\ late-2024 mean DIF$=$1.87, adjacent half-years mean DIF$=$0.94.\nAIC marginally favors IFT (48,921) over categorical MH (49,103), but deviance explained remains low ($R^2{=}0.031$).\n\n\\section{Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot}\n\\label{app:bifactor}\n\nWe attempted bifactor IRT modeling (general factor + 57 subject-specific factors) for fixed-parameter calibration equating.\nThe full 57-subject model failed to converge after 5,000 EM iterations.\nReduced models (10 and 5 subjects) converged but with poor fit: the 5-subject model yields RMSEA$=$0.078, CFI$=$0.724, TLI$=$0.698, SRMR$=$0.091---insufficient for reliable equating.\nFrom the 5-subject model, 312 candidate anchor items were identified (DIF A-class, discrimination $>$0.8), covering 38 of 57 subjects.\nThe equating pilot was abandoned; within-subject DIF analysis (C\\%$=$38.1\\%) provides an alternative validation path that does not require full IRT model specification.\n\n\\section{Not-Executed Experiments}\n\\label{app:not_executed}\n\nThree experiments were not executed due to resource constraints:\n\\emph{ConStat perturbation evaluation} (est.\\ 80 GPU-hours): would reformulate 250 MMLU items and evaluate perturbation-based detection.\n\\emph{DVD generation variance evaluation} (est.\\ 70 GPU-hours): would collect 50 responses per item across 12 models.\n\\emph{Ensemble fusion}: dropped after ConStat and DVD experiments were not executed; the paper pivoted from ensemble detection to diagnostic framing.\n\\emph{Item generation calibration}: blocked by bifactor IRT failure---without a reliable equating framework, replacement items cannot be calibrated to the same scale.\nThese remain directions for future work.\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
